---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {systems,systemPath,codesForSystem} from '../../lib/cars';
---
<Base title="Automotive code formats and reference scope" description="Understand how ErrHive Cars separates code families, generic references, manufacturer labels and exact vehicle documentation." breadcrumbs={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Code formats'}]}><article class="container prose-page"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">THE CHARACTERS MATTER</p><h1>One display needs context.</h1><p class="lede">Copy every character from the scanner. Do not replace letters with similar-looking numbers or discard the reporting module.</p></header><div class="prose"><section><h2>Code families in this library</h2><ul>{systems.map(system=><li><strong>{system.letter} — {system.name}.</strong> {system.description} {codesForSystem(system.id).length>0&&<a href={systemPath(system.id)}>Browse references</a>}</li>)}</ul></section><section><h2>Generic and manufacturer-labelled references</h2><p>A generic entry is stored once. Manufacturer-labelled entries preserve the labels and scope of their source. Neither kind of entry alone establishes compatibility with a particular model, year, engine or market.</p></section><section><h2>Suffixes and native displays</h2><p>A scanner can include extra characters or a manufacturer-native display. The code finder can locate a recognized base code, but its lookup is not a verification of the suffix or module-specific meaning. If the full display has no match, consult the exact vehicle documentation.</p></section><section><h2>A definition is not a failed-part verdict</h2><p>A reference describes a reported condition. Use the documented diagnostic procedure before treating it as evidence that a particular component needs replacement.</p></section><p><a href="/search/">Search the full display</a> · <a href="/sources/">Read the source labels</a></p></div></article></Base>
